{"name":"Tea Rose","hex":"#f883c2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tea rose","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of a Chinese rose species with tea-scented flowers, Rosa odorata.","A flower from such a plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tea_rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tea Rose","blocks":[{"text":"Tea Rose is an Australian fashion label. Founded in 1981 by designer Rosemary Armstrong, the company specializes in formal wear."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tea_Rose","wikidata":"Q7691097"},{"title":"Tea Rose (fragrance)","blocks":[{"text":"Tea Rose is a green rose soliflore fragrance that was released in 1973 by The Perfumer's Workshop. It was the first fragrance to use the scents of Bulgarian rose oil from iso-damascone."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tea_Rose_(fragrance)","wikidata":"Q141404817"},{"title":"Garden roses","blocks":[{"text":"Garden roses are hybrid roses that are grown as ornamental plants in private or public gardens. They are one of the most popular and widely cultivated groups of flowering plants, especially in temperate climates. An enormous number of garden cultivars has been produced, especially over the last two centuries, though roses have been known in the garden for millennia beforehand. While most garden roses are grown for their flowers, often in dedicated rose gardens, some are also valued for other reasons, such as having ornamental fruit, providing ground cover, or for hedging."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Rosa_Peace_1945.jpg/330px-Rosa_Peace_1945.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_roses","wikidata":"Q64140922"},{"title":"Tea","blocks":[{"text":"Tea is an aromatic beverage prepared by infusion of hot or boiling fresh water with fresh or cured leaves of Camellia sinensis, an evergreen shrub native to East Asia that originated in the borderlands of southwestern China, northeast India and northern Myanmar. Tea has a stimulant effect in humans primarily due to its caffeine content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Longjing_tea_steeping_in_gaiwan.jpg/330px-Longjing_tea_steeping_in_gaiwan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tea","wikidata":"Q6097"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Rosa_American_Pillar_-_Bagatelle01.jpg/500px-Rosa_American_Pillar_-_Bagatelle01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Rosa_American_Pillar_-_Bagatelle01.jpg/1280px-Rosa_American_Pillar_-_Bagatelle01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Rosa_American_Pillar_-_Bagatelle01.jpg/500px-Rosa_American_Pillar_-_Bagatelle01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The climber 'American Pillar', trained over a pergola","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARosa_American_Pillar_-_Bagatelle01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Rose_double.jpg/500px-Rose_double.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Rose_double.jpg/1280px-Rose_double.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Rose_double.jpg/500px-Rose_double.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Double delight\" rose","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARose_double.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Rose_Garden_View_in_Ooty%2C_Tamil_Nadu.JPG/500px-Rose_Garden_View_in_O